From 38af734ad4235baf64fad4da3faf7c3db063fdd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:07:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] core: stop FPS and ISO step lists at the last listed
 value under the cap

Why: selectableValueSteps added the mode's ceiling as an extra step
whenever a listed value lay above it. On IMX477 4144x2176 the FPS dial
went 24, 25, 30, 41.08 instead of 24, 25, 30, and buttons and encoders
stepped onto the odd ceiling with no way to turn it off. Original
Apertar (availablePcfFpsOptions, src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp:1150-1167 at
upstream-1.6.0) keeps the presets at or under the maximum and adds the
maximum only when no preset fits.

What: selectableValueSteps keeps the values at or under the cap and
pushes the cap only when that list is empty; the "above" flag is gone.
This applies to the FPS list (availablePcfFpsOptions) and the ISO list
(pcfIsoOptions). An operator who wants the ceiling as a step adds it to
the list in UI.conf.

Tested: check_core.sh lane-core1 both tests (PASS, no new warnings).
Copied the function into a host program: cap 41.08 -> 24 25 30,
cap 20 -> 20, cap 60 -> 24..60, cap 0 -> list unchanged. --simulate:
list_actions values.fps is 24 25 30 50 60. Needs the camera: IMX477 at
4144x2176, turn the FPS encoder to the top, it stops at 30; and
echo '{"cmd":"list_actions"}' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/apertar-core.sock
shows "fps":[24,25,30].

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NEFc1w7Y8dcyiV489BiwMr
---
 src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp | 9 +++------
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp b/src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp
index dfa2542..8293514 100644
--- a/src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp
+++ b/src/ApertarCoreApp.cpp
@@ -1269,21 +1269,18 @@ size_t nextPcfPresetIndex(size_t currentIndex, size_t count, PcfSettingStepMode
     return currentIndex;
 }
 
-// T33 (request 24): a list works on any sensor. The values up to the mode's ceiling are
-// selectable, and the ceiling itself is added when a value lies above it (cap 0 = none).
+// A list works on any sensor. The values up to the mode's ceiling are selectable; the ceiling
+// itself is the only step when no listed value fits (cap 0 = none).
 std::vector<double> selectableValueSteps(const std::vector<double> &steps, double cap)
 {
     if (!(cap > 0.0))
         return steps;
     std::vector<double> selectable;
-    bool above = false;
     for (double value : steps) {
         if (value <= cap + 0.0005)
             selectable.push_back(value);
-        else
-            above = true;
     }
-    if ((above || selectable.empty()) && (selectable.empty() || std::abs(selectable.back() - cap) > 0.0005))
+    if (selectable.empty())
         selectable.push_back(cap);
     return selectable;
 }
